1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 2.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,050
3, Remote Management.

3
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:07,300
So far you have worked at each server's
own console.

4
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:09,550
Real admins rarely do that.

5
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:15,000
You will learn why we manage servers
remotely, and how in a workgroup.

6
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:20,850
You will prepare SRV01 with a TrustedHosts
list, add SRV-CORE to Server Manager, and

7
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:23,900
run commands on it with PowerShell
remoting.

8
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:28,550
Then you will turn on OpenSSH Server and
connect with ssh.

9
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:35,200
Finally, you will use Remote Desktop and
learn to pick the right tool for each job.

10
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:40,650
Picture one seat, say SRV01 or CLIENT01,
with arrows to every other server.

11
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:44,900
The servers stay in their racks or run as
VMs.

12
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:47,950
There are three good reasons for this.

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,000
First, one console can manage many
servers.

14
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:55,650
Server Manager, PowerShell and Windows
Admin Center all work that way.

15
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:02,300
Second, Server Core has no desktop, so
remote tools give it a GUI or a full

16
00:01:02,550 --> 00:01:03,750
PowerShell.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,800
Third, fewer visits to the console means
better physical security and fewer

18
00:01:09,050 --> 00:01:11,050
accidents in the server room.

19
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:12,900
Here is the catch.

20
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:17,950
All the computers are in a workgroup, so
there is no Kerberos.

21
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:23,800
In a domain, Kerberos proves who both
computers are, and you just use names.

22
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:30,050
Without it, Windows falls back to NTLM,
and it will only send your credentials to

23
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,500
computers you trust.

24
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:36,950
So on SRV01, the computer you manage from,
you list SRV-CORE in TrustedHosts.

25
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,800
You also pass SRV-CORE's own Administrator
account each time.

26
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:47,050
The good news: remote management is on by
default and answers the local subnet, so

27
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:49,300
the firewall needs no change.

28
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:51,550
Two quick jobs on SRV01.

29
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,000
First, name lookup.

30
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:59,250
Without DNS, name lookup is not reliable,
and Server Manager works best with a name

31
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:01,500
rather than an IP address.

32
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:07,750
So you open the hosts file in Notepad and
add one line: the address 192.

33
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,200
168.

34
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:10,650
50.

35
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,700
21, some spaces, and the name SRV-CORE.

36
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:16,750
Test-Connection by name should now say
True.

37
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:18,200
Second, TrustedHosts.

38
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:20,050
Read the list first.

39
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:25,100
Then use Set-Item with the value SRV-CORE,
dash Concatenate and dash Force.

40
00:02:25,350 --> 00:02:27,350
Concatenate adds instead of replacing.

41
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:30,400
Read the value back to check it.

42
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:32,250
SRV-CORE itself needs nothing.

43
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:33,700
Now Server Manager.

44
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:35,950
Select Manage, then Add Servers.

45
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:40,200
The dialog has three tabs: Active
Directory, DNS and Import.

46
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:46,850
Active Directory only helps in a domain,
so in the pod you use the DNS tab.

47
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:50,700
Type SRV-CORE in the search box and select
Search.

48
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:54,150
Our hosts file line lets it find 192.

49
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,600
168.

50
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:57,050
50.

51
00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:58,500
21.

52
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:04,750
Select the server, then use the arrow
button to move it to the Selected list.

53
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,200
Select OK, and SRV-CORE joins the server
pool.

54
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:12,050
From now on, SRV01's Server Manager
watches both servers.

55
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:15,900
Select All Servers, and look at the
Manageability column.

56
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:20,550
If both Administrator accounts have the
same password, SRV-CORE already says

57
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,000
Online.

58
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:27,850
If not, you see an access error, because
Server Manager tried SRV01's own account.

59
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:31,300
Either way, right-click SRV-CORE and
choose Manage As.

60
00:03:31,550 --> 00:03:35,950
Type SRV-CORE backslash Administrator, and
the password shown in your pod.

61
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:40,200
That name means the Administrator account
that lives on SRV-CORE.

62
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:42,450
That makes the credentials explicit.

63
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,500
Select Refresh, and the status shows
Online.

64
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:48,150
Server Manager keeps them on SRV01.

65
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:53,200
Server Manager talks to SRV-CORE over
WinRM, and PowerShell remoting uses the

66
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:54,650
same connection.

67
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:58,900
First, run Get-Credential with SRV-CORE
backslash Administrator, and store the

68
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:01,550
result in a variable called cred.

69
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:05,000
Then run Enter-PSSession, dash
ComputerName SRV-CORE, dash Credential,

70
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:06,450
and the variable.

71
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:07,900
Watch the prompt.

72
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:11,350
It now starts with SRV-CORE in square
brackets.

73
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:14,000
Everything you type runs on SRV-CORE.

74
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:19,450
Here, hostname proves it, and Get-Service
shows that sshd is installed but stopped.

75
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:23,700
Exit-PSSession, or just exit, takes you
home to SRV01 again.

76
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:27,950
Sometimes you need just one answer, not a
whole session.

77
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:29,400
That is Invoke-Command.

78
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:35,250
You give it the computer name, the
credential, and a script block in curly

79
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:36,700
braces.

80
00:04:36,950 --> 00:04:42,950
The script block runs on SRV-CORE, and the
result comes back to your own prompt.

81
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:44,800
You never leave SRV01.

82
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:51,450
Ask for hostname, or for the time zone Id,
and you see the answer at once.

83
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:55,300
Add more names, and one command reaches
many servers.

84
00:04:55,550 --> 00:04:58,750
Many cmdlets, like Install-WindowsFeature,
also take dash ComputerName.

85
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,600
That is what Server Manager does behind
the scenes.

86
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:04,050
Next, SSH.

87
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:08,700
Starting with Windows Server 2025, OpenSSH
Server is installed by default.

88
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:10,950
It is just not running.

89
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:16,800
On a Desktop Experience server, Server
Manager's Local Server page has a new line

90
00:05:17,050 --> 00:05:18,650
called Remote SSH Access.

91
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,300
Select it to enable the service.

92
00:05:21,550 --> 00:05:27,950
The service name is sshd, it listens on
TCP port 22, and its firewall rule is

93
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:30,200
called OpenSSH Server In TCP.

94
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:34,450
SRV-CORE has no Server Manager window, so
we use PowerShell.

95
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:37,900
Older servers add it as an optional
feature.

96
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:42,150
On SRV-CORE, use option fifteen in SConfig
to reach PowerShell.

97
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:44,400
Set the startup type first.

98
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:47,450
Set-Service, dash Name sshd, dash
StartupType Automatic.

99
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:48,900
Why first?

100
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:54,750
A service whose startup type is Disabled
cannot start at all, so order matters

101
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,200
here.

102
00:05:56,450 --> 00:05:57,650
Then Start-Service sshd.

103
00:05:57,900 --> 00:05:59,900
No output means it worked.

104
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:04,550
Get-Service with Select-Object shows
Running and Automatic, so it will come

105
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:06,400
back after a restart.

106
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:09,450
Last, check the firewall rule with
Get-NetFirewallRule.

107
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:13,700
It should show Enabled True, so port 22 is
open.

108
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:18,350
If the rule is missing, create it as the
lab shows.

109
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:21,400
Now go to CLIENT01 and open PowerShell.

110
00:06:21,650 --> 00:06:23,650
Type ssh administrator at 192.

111
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:25,100
168.

112
00:06:25,350 --> 00:06:26,550
50.

113
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:28,000
21.

114
00:06:28,250 --> 00:06:33,450
The first time, ssh says it cannot
establish the authenticity of the host,

115
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:35,700
and shows a key fingerprint.

116
00:06:35,950 --> 00:06:38,750
Type yes, and CLIENT01 saves that key.

117
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,600
Then type the password.

118
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:44,850
Nothing shows while you type, and that is
on purpose.

119
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:46,700
Look at the prompt.

120
00:06:46,950 --> 00:06:51,750
There is no PS in front, because the
default shell is cmd.

121
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,600
You can type powershell, or set a registry
value called DefaultShell on the server,

122
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:00,650
which you will do in the lab.

123
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:04,100
Remote Desktop has been on since Lab 1.

124
00:07:04,350 --> 00:07:05,550
4.

125
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:11,000
On the Remote tab of System Properties,
Allow remote connections is selected, and

126
00:07:11,250 --> 00:07:15,250
that also opened the firewall rules for
TCP port 3389.

127
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,500
Keep Network Level Authentication on.

128
00:07:17,750 --> 00:07:24,150
With it, you sign in before a session
starts, so attackers do not even get a

129
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:25,600
sign-in screen.

130
00:07:25,850 --> 00:07:27,050
Who may connect?

131
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:31,700
Members of Administrators, plus members of
the Remote Desktop Users group.

132
00:07:31,950 --> 00:07:34,750
Select Users adds people to that group.

133
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,800
Right now Get-LocalGroupMember shows it is
empty.

134
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:41,650
On CLIENT01, type mstsc to open Remote
Desktop Connection.

135
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:45,100
In the Computer box, type SRV01's address,
192.

136
00:07:45,350 --> 00:07:46,550
168.

137
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:48,000
50.

138
00:07:48,250 --> 00:07:49,450
20.

139
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:52,900
A name will work once we have DNS.

140
00:07:53,150 --> 00:07:54,350
Select Connect.

141
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:59,800
In a workgroup, sign in as SRV01 backslash
Administrator, not as CLIENT01 backslash

142
00:08:00,050 --> 00:08:01,250
student.

143
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:07,500
You will see a warning that the identity
of the remote computer cannot be verified.

144
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:12,150
SRV01 uses a self-signed certificate, so
select Yes in the pod.

145
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:18,800
mstsc also takes options, like slash v for
the computer, slash f for full screen, and

146
00:08:19,050 --> 00:08:20,250
slash admin.

147
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:23,300
When you finish, how do you leave?

148
00:08:23,550 --> 00:08:27,950
If you close the window with the X, you
only disconnect.

149
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:33,000
Your session keeps running on the server,
with every program still open.

150
00:08:33,250 --> 00:08:38,850
If you select Start, your user icon, and
Sign out, the session ends, programs

151
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:41,500
close, and the memory is freed.

152
00:08:41,750 --> 00:08:44,150
Forgotten sessions can block a colleague.

153
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:49,600
Remote Desktop to Server Core shows the
console window, and SConfig opens, unless

154
00:08:49,850 --> 00:08:53,450
you take over a session that is already
open.

155
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:57,300
Option twelve in SConfig, or logoff, signs
you out.

156
00:08:57,550 --> 00:08:59,150
So which tool when?

157
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:05,400
Server Manager uses WinRM on port 5985 and
is great for watching many servers and

158
00:09:05,650 --> 00:09:06,850
adding roles.

159
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:13,100
Windows Admin Center runs in a browser,
talking HTTPS to the gateway on SRV01, and

160
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:15,750
gives Server Core a web GUI.

161
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,600
PowerShell remoting also uses WinRM, and
is best for commands on one or many

162
00:09:21,850 --> 00:09:23,050
servers.

163
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:27,300
SSH uses port 22 and works from any
operating system.

164
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:31,150
Remote Desktop uses port 3389 for a full
desktop.

165
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:32,600
Many servers?

166
00:09:32,850 --> 00:09:34,050
PowerShell.

167
00:09:34,300 --> 00:09:35,500
Only a desktop?

168
00:09:35,750 --> 00:09:36,950
Remote Desktop.

169
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:38,800
Four mistakes to avoid.

170
00:09:39,050 --> 00:09:41,050
One: setting TrustedHosts on SRV-CORE.

171
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:45,300
The list belongs on SRV01, the computer
you manage from.

172
00:09:45,550 --> 00:09:50,750
Two: using the wrong account with Manage
As, like CLIENT01 backslash student or

173
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,200
SRV01 backslash Administrator.

174
00:09:52,450 --> 00:09:55,650
Use the target's own account, SRV-CORE
backslash Administrator.

175
00:09:55,900 --> 00:09:57,500
Three: Start-Service sshd fails.

176
00:09:57,750 --> 00:10:02,550
If the startup type is Disabled,
Start-Service fails, so set it to

177
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:04,000
Automatic first.

178
00:10:04,250 --> 00:10:08,650
Four: closing the Remote Desktop window
and thinking you are done.

179
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:10,500
That only disconnects you.

180
00:10:10,750 --> 00:10:13,550
Sign out so the session really ends.

181
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:15,400
Time to check yourself.

182
00:10:15,650 --> 00:10:18,850
You manage the pod from SRV01 and
CLIENT01.

183
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:22,700
First, Enter-PSSession to SRV-CORE fails
with a TrustedHosts error.

184
00:10:22,950 --> 00:10:25,750
Which computer do you fix, and how?

185
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,200
Second, your prompt shows SRV-CORE in
square brackets.

186
00:10:29,450 --> 00:10:31,050
Where does Get-Service run?

187
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:35,700
Third, ssh connects, but the prompt has no
PS in front.

188
00:10:35,950 --> 00:10:38,750
Why, and how do you get PowerShell?

189
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:45,000
And fourth, a help-desk user needs Remote
Desktop to SRV01 but must not be an

190
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:46,450
administrator.

191
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:48,300
What do you do?

192
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:51,350
Pause the video and think it through.

193
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:53,200
Here are the answers.

194
00:10:53,450 --> 00:10:56,650
One: fix SRV01, the computer you manage
from.

195
00:10:56,900 --> 00:11:02,100
Use Set-Item on the TrustedHosts path with
the value SRV-CORE, then pass dash

196
00:11:02,350 --> 00:11:03,550
Credential.

197
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,800
Two: it runs on SRV-CORE.

198
00:11:06,050 --> 00:11:11,250
The prefix in square brackets means every
command runs there until you type

199
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:12,700
Exit-PSSession.

200
00:11:12,950 --> 00:11:15,750
Three: the default SSH shell is cmd.

201
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,800
Type powershell, or set the DefaultShell
value in the OpenSSH registry key.

202
00:11:21,050 --> 00:11:26,650
Four: add the user to Remote Desktop
Users, with Select Users on the Remote

203
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:28,100
tab.

204
00:11:28,350 --> 00:11:31,550
They do not need to be an administrator.

205
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:38,600
On SRV01, I add the SRV-CORE line to the
hosts file, and the ping by name says

206
00:11:38,850 --> 00:11:40,050
True.

207
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:43,100
I add SRV-CORE to TrustedHosts with
Concatenate.

208
00:11:43,350 --> 00:11:47,350
In Server Manager I add SRV-CORE from the
DNS tab.

209
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:53,600
If it shows an access error, use Manage
As; either way, setting Manage As to

210
00:11:53,850 --> 00:11:56,650
SRV-CORE backslash Administrator makes the
credentials explicit.

211
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:59,300
I refresh, and it is Online.

212
00:11:59,550 --> 00:12:04,750
In a session on SRV-CORE, I set sshd to
Automatic and start it.

213
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:11,800
On CLIENT01, ssh asks about the key, I say
yes, type the password, and I am in.

214
00:12:12,050 --> 00:12:15,250
Last, Remote Desktop to SRV01, then sign
out.

215
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:16,700
To wrap up.

216
00:12:16,950 --> 00:12:21,750
In a workgroup, remote management needs
three things: name lookup, SRV-CORE in

217
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,200
TrustedHosts on SRV01, and the target's
own credentials.

218
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:29,450
Server Manager adds servers with Add
Servers and Manage As.

219
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:33,700
Enter-PSSession gives you a session, and
Invoke-Command runs one command.

220
00:12:33,950 --> 00:12:38,350
OpenSSH comes with Server 2025: start
sshd, and use port 22.

221
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:43,800
For Remote Desktop, keep NLA on, use
Remote Desktop Users, and sign out.

222
00:12:44,050 --> 00:12:45,250
Lab 2.

223
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:49,100
3 practices all of this in about fifty
minutes.

224
00:12:49,350 --> 00:12:50,950
It is practice only.

225
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:53,200
See you in the lab.
